  "issue_title": "results_table: multi-task summary crashes with TypeError when an eval omits random_routing",
  "issue_body": "## Bug\n\n`scripts/results_table.py` treats a missing/null score as a first-class case *everywhere except* the multi-task summary, where it crashes.\n\n`load_rows()` reads both headline baselines with `.get()`, so either can legitimately be `None`:\n\n```python\n\"trinity\": r.get(\"TRINITY\"),\n\"random\": r.get(\"random_routing\"),\n```\n\nThe per-coordinator table is written defensively against exactly that \u2014 it coerces with `or 0` (lines 59-60) and `fmt()` renders a non-numeric score as `\u2014` (line 48). But the multi-task summary sums and maxes those same values raw:\n\n```python\ntrin_avg = sum(max(r[\"trinity\"] for r in by_bench[b]) for b in benches) / len(benches)\nrand_avg = sum(\n    sum(r[\"random\"] for r in by_bench[b]) / len(by_bench[b]) for b in benches\n) / len(benches)\n```\n\n`sum()`/`max()` over a `None` raises `TypeError`, aborting the whole report.\n\nNote `single_avg()` (lines 75-85), directly above, *does* handle this correctly: it filters `None` out per benchmark and returns `None` when a model has no usable score. The TRINITY/random aggregates were simply never given the same treatment.\n\n## Reproduce\n\nAny run where at least two benchmarks are present (so the `len(benches) >= 2` summary branch executes) and one `eval*.json` omits `random_routing` \u2014 e.g. an older eval, a hand-trimmed JSON, or an eval whose random-routing baseline was skipped:\n\n```python\nrows = [\n    row(\"math500\", \"c1\", trinity=0.85, random=None, single=0.70),\n    row(\"mmlu\",    \"c1\", trinity=0.85, random=0.50, single=0.80),\n]\nrender(rows)\n```\n\n```\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'int' and 'NoneType'\n```\n\nThe same crash occurs if any `TRINITY` value is null. With a single benchmark the summary is skipped, so the per-row table renders `\u2014` and the bug stays hidden \u2014 it only fires on exactly the multi-task report that produces the paper's headline R1/R2/R4 verdicts.\n\n## Impact\n\n`python scripts/results_table.py` dies with a `TypeError` and produces **no report at all** \u2014 not even the per-coordinator table it had already built \u2014 because one eval file is missing an optional baseline. `--json` never writes `experiments/results.json`.\n\nThe tempting one-line \"fix\" (`r[\"random\"] or 0`, mirroring line 60) would be **worse than the crash**: a missing random baseline would silently become `0.000`, and `R4 (TRINITY avg > random avg)` would print `\u2705 HOLDS` against a baseline that was never measured. A missing measurement must not be reported as a won comparison.\n\n## Expected\n\nThe multi-task summary should aggregate `None` the way `single_avg()` already does:\n\n- Drop `None` scores per benchmark before taking the per-bench `max` (TRINITY) / `mean` (random), keeping the existing aggregation semantics for the values that *are* present.\n- When a baseline has no usable scores anywhere, report it as `\u2014` and mark its invariant verdict as not evaluable, rather than crashing or fabricating a `0.000` comparison.\n\n## Location\n\n`scripts/results_table.py`, `render()`, lines 87-90 (and the R4 verdict on line 104).\n",
  "merged_at": "2026-07-12T06:20:37.000Z",
